Each letter of "snuke" fixes its position in the cycle, so a cell's letter tells us exactly where it must appear in the sequence. For a cell with letter at index k in "snuke", the next cell must hold the letter at index (k+1) mod 5. Letters not in "snuke" can never be visited.

This turns the problem into reachability in a directed graph: an edge goes from a cell to an adjacent cell only if the adjacent cell has the required next letter. A BFS from (1,1) answers whether (H,W) is reachable. The grid has at most 250,000 cells, so a simple BFS over a flattened grid is fast enough.

```python
import sys
from collections import deque
def main():
    data=sys.stdin.read().split()
    H=int(data[0]);W=int(data[1])
    S=data[2:2+H]
    pat="snuke"
    idx={c:i for i,c in enumerate(pat)}
    g=[-1]*(H*W)
    for i in range(H):
        row=S[i]
        for j in range(W):
            g[i*W+j]=idx.get(row[j],-1)
    if g[0]!=0:
        print("No");return
    vis=bytearray(H*W)
    vis[0]=1
    dq=deque([0])
    target=H*W-1
    while dq:
        p=dq.popleft()
        if p==target:
            print("Yes");return
        nk=(g[p]+1)%5
        i,j=divmod(p,W)
        if i>0:
            q=p-W
            if not vis[q] and g[q]==nk:
                vis[q]=1;dq.append(q)
        if i<H-1:
            q=p+W
            if not vis[q] and g[q]==nk:
                vis[q]=1;dq.append(q)
        if j>0:
            q=p-1
            if not vis[q] and g[q]==nk:
                vis[q]=1;dq.append(q)
        if j<W-1:
            q=p+1
