The idea is to sort the elements inside each tuple, so tuples with the same elements in a different order become identical. Then a set intersection finds the tuples common to both lists.

```python
def tuple_intersection(test_list1, test_list2):
    """
    Find the intersection of two lists of tuples, ignoring the order
    of elements within each tuple.

    Each tuple is normalized by sorting its elements, so (5, 4) and (4, 5)
    are treated as the same tuple. Returns a set of normalized tuples.
    """
    set1 = {tuple(sorted(t)) for t in test_list1}
    set2 = {tuple(sorted(t)) for t in test_list2}
    return set1 & set2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    assert tuple_intersection([(3, 4), (5, 6), (9, 10), (4, 5)],
                              [(5, 4), (3, 4), (6, 5), (9, 11)]) == {(4, 5), (3, 4), (5, 6)}
    assert tuple_intersection([(4, 1), (7, 4), (11, 13), (17, 14)],
                              [(1, 4), (7, 4), (16, 12), (10, 13)]) == {(4, 7), (1, 4)}
    assert tuple_intersection([(2, 1), (3, 2), (1, 3), (1, 4)],
                              [(11, 2), (2, 3), (6, 2), (1, 3)]) == {(1, 3), (2, 3)}
    print("All tests passed!")
```

The result holds the sorted form of each common tuple. For example, `(4, 5)` and `(5, 4)` both appear as `(4, 5)`.

