    ) == [{'name': 'Item-2', 'price': 555.22}]

    assert expensive_items(
        [{'name': 'Item-1', 'price': 101.1}, {'name': 'Item-2', 'price': 555.22},
         {'name': 'Item-3', 'price': 45.09}], 2
    ) == [{'name': 'Item-2', 'price': 555.22}, {'name': 'Item-1', 'price': 101.1}]

    assert expensive_items(
        [{'name': 'Item-1', 'price': 101.1}, {'name': 'Item-2', 'price': 555.22},
         {'name': 'Item-3', 'price': 45.09}, {'name': 'Item-4', 'price': 22.75}], 1
    ) == [{'name': 'Item-2', 'price': 555.22}]

    print("All tests passed!")
```

The function uses `heapq.nlargest` with the `price` field as the sort key. It returns the top `n` items in descending order of price and runs in O(len(items) · log n) time.

